 - **City-counter role pruning (e.g. Macemen)**: Removed `UNITAI_CITY_COUNTER` from **non-versatile** units (e.g., `UNIT_MACEMAN`). For example for macemen, as city defenders they’re too easy to punish—knights counter them hard, and even crossbow stacks can beat defending macemen—so they’re better assigned to **offensive** `UnitAI` roles. While macemen beat pikemen, pikes seldom assault cities efficiently, so the city-counter slot isn’t warranted. The same adjustment applies to any other units with this mismatch.
 - **Mounted “Counter” role pruning:** Removed `UNITAI_COUNTER` from the mounted units (`UNIT_CHARIOT`, `UNIT_EGYPTIAN_WAR_CHARIOT`, `UNIT_CARTHAGE_NUMIDIAN_CAVALRY`) that had it. Rationale: mounted units don’t "receive defense bonuses" (note: not related to bonuses/ressources) and can be countered by other units; assigning them to COUNTER can park them in cities (e.g. seemingly seen in autoplay with War Chariots) and occupies a slot **Longbowmen** are more fit to fill as they unlock early and are cheap and versatile defenders, so these mounted unit lines are better used for offense roles rather during its window—or skipped outside it.
-- **Very cheap-combat spam cap (tiered & generalized):** The AI was **overbuilding very cheap combat units**—notably the **Ancient Maceman**—even ~T100. Because they’re so cheap, they pile up, are weak once Archers appear, and can drag the economy. We added conditions in `CvCityAI::AI_chooseUnit` to **limit** these builds, with a **stricter cap after ~T50** when they’re especially poor value. In AdvCiv-SAS, Archers arrive early, are cheap/versatile, and fill defense quotas sooner, letting cities pivot to offense. (Scouts get trimmed incidentally since they’re combat-tagged, though they weren’t the main issue.) **Result:** more varied, stronger stacks (e.g., ~3 Ancient Macemen @T100–130 vs ~11 before) and a **healthier economy**. See [KI#53.4](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs_And_Appendixes/README_Known_Issues_In_Base_AdvCiv_Civ4.md#534-tremendously-improved-ai-overbuilding-very-cheap-combat-units-ancient-macemen-only-being-an-issue-as-of-now-anyways-etc-in-the-early-game-sometimes-even-at-turn-100-which-is-inefficient-and-easy-to-overshoot-if-i-may-say-but-anyways-etc-as-they-are-cheap-and-accumulate-quickly-but-are-not-too-effective-especially-as-soon-as-we-get-archer-units-anyways-etc-now-limited-especially-even-more-so-after-as-of-now-turn-50-where-they-should-be-especially-useless-much-better-military-compositions-and-better-growth-as-a-result-anyways-etc). **Update:** The cap in `CvCityAI::AI_chooseUnit` is now **generalized beyond Ancient** using **tier-based (not era-based) thresholds** to avoid era bleed-through (e.g., Ancient Macemen don’t inherit looser Classical caps). This also aims to trim (at least in theory, data lacking to draw a conclusion on an experiment anyways etc) for example **Archers in Classical** and **Longbows/Triremes in Medieval** when they’re too cheap for their tier. In practice we see AI mixes improve (e.g., Japan AI shifting from ~75 Longbows @T200 to ~50–60 Longbows + 10–15 Camel Archers + 20–27 Samurai, run-dependent). **Update (fallback production):** Applied the same cap inside `CvCity::doTurn`’s **no-production fallback** so that the **new offense-only** alternative logic no longer spams **Ancient Macemen** (e.g., ~19 @T100). **Update 2 (Renaissance+ anti-pike / cheap melee):** Added extra gating for **Renaissance+ very-cheap land, direct-combat units** (no scouts, spies, naval units, etc.). Goal: reduce ineffective late-game spam of obsolete counters (e.g. pikes into guns) and improve economic efficiency (lower unit costs, with more hammers available for stronger units and/or additional buildings instead of obsolete high-maintenance units).
-- **Great Generals — prefer Military Instructor, focus on hammer hubs:** AI now favours settling Great Generals as **Military Instructors** over attaching them as unit leaders, as the instructor bonus scales better into the late game. **Targeting:** strong bias to the **top 3 hammer cities** with **decreasing weights** (top-1 most, then top-2/3 progressively less). Extra preference if the city **has / is building** the **Heroic Epic effect** building. **Limit removed:** cities may host **multiple** Military Instructors (no per-city cap). Implemented in `CvCityAI::AI_permanentSpecialistValue` and `CvUnitAI::AI_generalMove`. See [KI#69](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs_And_Appendixes/README_Known_Issues_In_Base_AdvCiv_Civ4.md#69---tremendously-improved-ai-going-for-great-general-leaders-while-military-instructors-are-much-better-with-added-logic-to-favour-top-hammer-cities-remove-military-instructor-per-city-limit-favour-it-further-if-have-or-building-heroic-epic-effect-building-etc). Note: sometimes/rarely ignored in autoplay.
-- (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 5277+) **Unit upgrades - stop wasting gold on tiny gains:** Fix AI tendency to **over-upgrade units** even when the upgrade is **poor value** (high gold cost for a small strength gain), which can burn many turns of **gold** and **slow tech pace**. Before allowing an upgrade, the AI must pass **simple ROI gates** based on **strength** gained vs gold cost: (1) gold per 10% strength gain, and (2) gold per +1 **flat strength** gain; both gates are **more lenient with higher unit EXP** (high-EXP units keep their promotions after being upgraded which is valuable, so spending more gold on them is more justified), while low-EXP units are treated more strictly (often better to let them die and rebuild if needed, unless it is cheap enough to upgrade them). Example (Knight -> Cuirassier): 10 strength -> 13 strength; if one of the gates requires `15 gold per +1 flat strength gain` with `+1 gold per +1 flat strength gain per 1 EXP`, then we won't upgrade the unit if its upgrade cost is more than `15 * 3 = 45 gold` at **0 EXP**, or `((15 + 18) * 3) = 99 gold` at **18 EXP** for this gate. In autoplay, this seemingly leads to **all AIs teching significantly faster**, especially in the **late game** where there are many units and AI would often have its tech pace stall for dozens of turns. Change in `CvUnit::canUpgrade` (single global gate, like `CvUnit::canScrap`); see [KI#88](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs_And_Appendixes/README_Known_Issues_In_Base_AdvCiv_Civ4.md#88---tremendously-improved-ai-always-upgrading-way-too-much-units-and-not-teching-at-all-sometimes-for-dozen-turns).
